1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,368
The school compares enteral, parenteral and intraosseous routes. Use
those categories to understand delivery, not as a ladder in which
every sick patient automatically moves to IV or IO.

2
00:00:13,368 --> 00:00:26,064
The route must fit the indication, formulation, patient and actual
authority. IV access has risks and may not be needed when an effective
less-invasive treatment is available.

3
00:00:26,064 --> 00:00:41,832
The school table lists IV or IO as preferred in anaphylaxis. That is
not the initial-care rule: current E zero nine prioritizes
intramuscular epinephrine. Do not delay indicated IM treatment while
seeking a line.

4
00:00:41,832 --> 00:00:51,864
Generic poor-perfusion concerns do not cancel that indication-specific
rule. State the treatment and route together, then check its current
clinical pathway.

5
00:00:51,864 --> 00:01:03,600
The intima is the inner endothelial lining; valves are folds of that
inner layer. Mechanical or chemical irritation can contribute to
inflammation and thrombosis.

6
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:15,456
The media includes smooth muscle and elastic tissue and is thinner
than the comparable arterial layer. Sympathetic tone can alter venous
calibre, particularly in cold or shock.

7
00:01:15,456 --> 00:01:24,384
The externa provides connective support. These are tissue layers, not
three separate lumens into which a needle may be placed
interchangeably.

8
00:01:24,384 --> 00:01:38,496
Veins generally return blood toward the heart at lower pressure and
hold substantial blood volume. Direction defines a vein; oxygen
content is not the universal definition, because pulmonary veins are
an important exception.

9
00:01:38,496 --> 00:01:49,728
Flow depends on catheter and tubing properties as well as the driving
pressure. A narrower or longer catheter generally offers more
resistance under comparable conditions.

10
00:01:49,728 --> 00:02:00,768
For gravity delivery, bag height relative to the site contributes
pressure. A kink, closed clamp, retained constricting band or bent
joint can restrict flow.

11
00:02:00,768 --> 00:02:11,280
A running drip chamber is not proof that the fluid is entering the
vein. Check the site and delivered volume, especially after movement
or a sudden rate change.

12
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:24,528
Choose access and equipment for the actual need rather than
automatically choosing the largest catheter or a universal proximal
site. Avoid unnecessary attempts and do not delay appropriate
transport.

13
00:02:24,528 --> 00:02:36,504
Shock or cold can reduce tissue perfusion and delay IM or SC
absorption. The finding should change reassessment and the clinical
plan, not justify an improvised second route.

14
00:02:36,504 --> 00:02:51,144
IV dosing bypasses absorption, but reaching the target and producing
effect still depend on circulation and pharmacodynamics. Direct access
does not make onset literally instantaneous for every medicine.

15
00:02:51,144 --> 00:03:04,584
IO is not a blanket solution for every collapsed vein, child or
conscious shocked patient. The current PCP conditions and age
restrictions apply even if a general physiology diagram looks
favourable.

16
00:03:04,584 --> 00:03:17,256
If a route is failing, support immediate breathing or circulatory
threats, identify why and request appropriate help. The correct
alternative must be clinically suitable and authorized.

17
00:03:17,256 --> 00:03:29,112
A swallowed medicine passes through the gastrointestinal system. A
suitable sublingual medicine is absorbed through mucosa; it does not
need to travel through the gut to produce that intended effect.

18
00:03:29,112 --> 00:03:38,688
The school groups PO and SL as enteral for teaching, but do not infer
identical absorption, first-pass handling or clinical suitability.

19
00:03:38,688 --> 00:03:52,560
Assess whether the patient can cooperate, manage secretions and
protect the airway, and whether active vomiting or distress prevents
safe use. Do not provoke gagging as a shortcut to certifying
swallowing safety.

20
00:03:52,560 --> 00:04:06,312
If the prescribed route is unsafe, hold and use the appropriate
authorized pathway. Crushing an unsuitable tablet, changing its route
or giving a liquid to an unprotected airway is not a safe workaround.

21
00:04:06,312 --> 00:04:20,304
A subcutaneous dose is deposited in tissue beneath the dermis and
above muscle. An intramuscular dose is deposited within the selected
muscle. The intended layer affects absorption and technique.

22
00:04:20,304 --> 00:04:33,960
Needle length, gauge, site, tissue thickness, medication and volume
must be selected together. A needle too short may miss muscle; a
poorly placed deep needle can injure underlying structures.

23
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:46,056
The diagram is a layer concept, not a body landmark map. Learn the
actual landmarks on a model with the instructor and verify the
specific device or medication procedure.

24
00:04:46,056 --> 00:04:56,328
Do not use the textbook's largest listed IM volume as the permitted
volume for every site or child. Site capacity and formulation
requirements differ.

25
00:04:56,328 --> 00:05:09,120
Confirm the specific indication and IM formulation before preparation.
IM is a preferred emergency route in some conditions, not merely a
backup whenever an IV was inconvenient.

26
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:23,616
Verify the dose, volume and appropriate equipment, and label the
prepared medication. Choose the site specified by the indication and
current procedure; anaphylaxis epinephrine uses the anterolateral mid-
thigh.

27
00:05:23,616 --> 00:05:35,760
Inspect skin and identify the actual landmarks rather than choosing an
arbitrary distance from the shoulder. Position the patient so the
intended muscle is accessible and relaxed.

28
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:49,584
Use hand hygiene, the indicated PPE, aseptic handling and an
accessible sharps container. The first live injection must be within
the school's supervised authorization process, not self-practice from
this slide.

29
00:05:49,584 --> 00:05:59,856
After the complete medication check, clean the intended site with the
approved preparation and let it dry. Avoid touching and contaminating
the prepared area.

30
00:05:59,856 --> 00:06:13,536
Using the instructed landmark and patient-specific equipment,
stabilize the tissue and enter the muscle with the taught technique,
generally at ninety degrees. Needle depth cannot be determined from
the angle alone.

31
00:06:13,536 --> 00:06:28,992
Follow the current medication and device procedure for aspiration and
delivery rate. The older textbook's routine aspiration rule is not
universal for all injections, and a vaccine rule is not automatically
a rule for every therapeutic drug.

32
00:06:28,992 --> 00:06:43,176
Withdraw safely, activate the safety device and immediately dispose
without recapping. Provide the indicated site care and document dose,
site, time and response, including any unexpected reaction.

33
00:06:43,176 --> 00:06:55,824
Verify that the medicine, volume and SC route are appropriate. Choose
an intact taught site and the device and needle that reach the
subcutaneous layer without unintentionally reaching muscle.

34
00:06:55,824 --> 00:07:09,624
SC angle depends on tissue and device, commonly forty-five to ninety
degrees in general teaching. The older Nancy passage uses forty-five
degrees throughout; that is not every current product's instruction.

35
00:07:09,624 --> 00:07:22,968
Do not universally aspirate or massage after a SC dose. The candidate
Canadian procedure says not to massage; actual aspiration and site
care must follow the medicine and current procedure.

36
00:07:22,968 --> 00:07:34,488
Dispose safely and monitor the site and patient. Poor perfusion may
delay absorption, and repeated doses need the actual route-specific
interval and cumulative check.

37
00:07:34,488 --> 00:07:46,176
Intranasal delivery requires an appropriate medication and device. A
syringe with a mucosal atomizer and a sealed unit-dose spray have
different preparation and delivery instructions.

38
00:07:46,176 --> 00:07:59,112
For the syringe procedure, verify concentration and calculated volume
before attaching the atomizer. Remove the fill tip safely and inspect
for nasal blood or mucus that could obstruct delivery.

39
00:07:59,112 --> 00:08:12,864
Current PR eleven accounts for the atomizer's zero-point-one-
millilitre dead space and prepares half-dose delivery to each nostril.
Follow that exact device procedure, not a remembered instruction from
a different spray.

40
00:08:12,864 --> 00:08:25,896
Coordinate access to the nose without unnecessarily interrupting life-
sustaining ventilation. Naloxone delivery is part of an airway-first
plan, not a reason to leave the patient unventilated.

41
00:08:25,896 --> 00:08:37,680
The current syringe-atomizer procedure limits volume to one millilitre
per nostril. A correct drug amount may be unsuitable intranasally if
its concentration requires too much volume.

42
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:50,160
For a fictional one-point-six-milligram dose at one milligram per
millilitre, the intended drug volume is one point six millilitres, or
zero point eight per nostril before device preparation.

43
00:08:50,160 --> 00:09:03,816
At half a milligram per millilitre, the same amount would need three
point two millilitres, or one point six per nostril, beyond that
limit. Do not solve it by flooding the nose or inventing repeat
intervals.

44
00:09:03,816 --> 00:09:17,304
Resolve the concentration or an authorized alternative with the
current pathway. Reassess actual delivery and clinical response; an
atomized dose is not proof that an exact systemic amount has been
absorbed.

45
00:09:17,304 --> 00:09:30,912
Verify the inhaler name, strength, remaining doses, expiry and prior
use. Prepare and shake or prime only as required by that product, and
inspect the spacer and valves.

46
00:09:30,912 --> 00:09:44,544
Use an effective mouthpiece or mask seal. Deliver one actuation at a
time into the spacer and coach the appropriate slow inhalation or
approved tidal-breathing technique for that patient and device.

47
00:09:44,544 --> 00:09:56,208
An able patient may hold the breath as comfortably instructed, but do
not require a distressed patient to perform an impossible ten-second
hold or stop needed oxygen unnecessarily.

48
00:09:56,208 --> 00:10:07,200
Repeat only within the indicated regimen and reassess. A correctly
counted number of sprays does not establish delivery if the seal
failed or the patient could not inhale.

49
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:20,832
Verify the intended drug and concentration and use the required
diluent and device. Flow requirements and fill volume depend on the
nebulizer; the textbook's single six-litre setting is not universal.

50
00:10:20,832 --> 00:10:33,312
Keep the chamber and interface in the intended orientation and coach
breathing that the patient can manage. A visible mist outside the mask
is not proof that the intended amount reaches the airways.

51
00:10:33,312 --> 00:10:49,128
Use the current infection-control and device precautions. For
ineffective breathing, ventilation support takes priority; a trained
compatible in-line system may be part of the plan, but must not
compromise BVM effectiveness.

52
00:10:49,128 --> 00:11:04,320
Reassess airflow, work, speech and the adverse response. Increasing
fatigue, decreased awareness or poor air movement requires urgent
escalation, not more time waiting beside a running nebulizer.

53
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:18,216
A line should have an actual indicated use. A vague expectation that
the hospital may want an IV later is not enough by itself, and an
effective less-invasive route may make a line unnecessary.

54
00:11:18,216 --> 00:11:28,944
Verify access authority as well as drug authority. Current BCEHS PCP
under-twelve cannulation restrictions apply even when a school model
demonstrates the procedure.

55
00:11:28,944 --> 00:11:41,688
Trace the established line to the patient and inspect the site,
solution, connectors and any other running treatment. Do not assume
patency from a label or a historic successful insertion.

56
00:11:41,688 --> 00:11:54,048
This week prepares IV-push medication checks; the following school
weekend develops access skills. Passing a preparation lesson does not
demonstrate actual cannulation competence.

57
00:11:54,048 --> 00:12:04,488
IV push means a syringe dose through appropriate vascular access. Some
drugs require slow delivery, so the word push does not establish
speed.

58
00:12:04,488 --> 00:12:18,000
Complete the medication and patient checks, including current
dilution, compatibility and administration time. Clean the connector
using the actual device and antiseptic procedure and let it dry as
required.

59
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:31,584
Confirm line identity and patency using the approved method. Pain,
swelling, leakage or resistance requires reassessment; do not force
the dose to make a difficult line work.

60
00:12:31,584 --> 00:12:46,176
Deliver at the drug-specific rate while observing the patient and
site. Device-specific clamping or connection technique must prevent
backflow and unintended delivery, not blindly reproduce an older
needle-port diagram.

61
00:12:46,176 --> 00:12:57,744
After syringe delivery, part of a dose may remain in the connector or
extension. The actual line dead space and compatible flush procedure
matter to what reaches the patient.

62
00:12:57,744 --> 00:13:10,200
Use the appropriate compatible flush volume and method for the device,
patient and medicine. The older textbook's twenty-millilitre flush is
not a universal rule for every age and access.

63
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:21,456
A rapid flush can rapidly deliver a slow-push drug still in dead
space. The medication-specific rate must be preserved where relevant,
including the residual dose.

64
00:13:21,456 --> 00:13:33,312
Do not automatically run the line wide open to clear it. Restore the
intended ongoing rate and document actual drug and relevant fluid
delivery, then reassess effect and harm.

65
00:13:33,312 --> 00:13:46,632
Fluid or medication outside the intended vessel may cause local
injury. Hyperosmolar products such as dextrose can be particularly
damaging; an IV label does not prevent tissue exposure.

66
00:13:46,632 --> 00:13:59,928
Watch for new pain, swelling, coolness, leakage, resistance or altered
flow, and compare the site with the patient's report. Not every
complication presents with every sign.

67
00:13:59,928 --> 00:14:12,456
Stop the implicated administration and follow the specific
extravasation procedure, preserving the device if the protocol needs
it for aspiration or treatment. Seek appropriate clinical guidance.

68
00:14:12,456 --> 00:14:25,968
Do not force a flush through the suspect site or improvise heat, cold,
massage or an antidote. Subsequent access and treatment depend on the
actual drug, tissue and authorized plan.

69
00:14:25,968 --> 00:14:37,200
The vascular marrow network connects with systemic venous circulation,
allowing indicated IO delivery when peripheral access cannot be
established in the permitted clinical setting.

70
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:48,960
The bony space resists collapse, but that does not guarantee correct
placement, flow or immediate peak drug effect. Placement and ongoing
tissue checks are still essential.

71
00:14:48,960 --> 00:15:02,280
Complications include painful infusion, extravasation, compartment
injury, infection and device movement. A conscious patient's pain plan
requires the appropriately authorized practitioner.

72
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:14,232
For PCP, review the exact current PR twelve pathway from the
pharmacology lecture. Do not choose IO solely because a generic school
case describes severe dehydration.

73
00:15:14,232 --> 00:15:26,016
One school subsection lists proximal humerus as a recommended PCP IO
site. The checked current BCEHS procedure explicitly restricts PCP
placement to a tibial site.

74
00:15:26,016 --> 00:15:39,456
The same source supplies generic needle lengths and a ten-to-twenty-
millilitre saline-plus-lidocaine instruction. Do not treat those as a
patient-specific device selection or an unrestricted local-anesthetic
order.

75
00:15:39,456 --> 00:15:53,856
Use current patient, site, authority and manufacturer criteria.
Confirm placement and protect the site using the actual taught
procedure; a single reassuring sign is not a guarantee against
extravasation.

76
00:15:53,856 --> 00:16:09,072
The saved subsection also contains unrelated template prose. It is not
a clinical instruction or a learning objective. The course uses
substantive clinical content and identifies corrections instead of
filling slides with that text.

77
00:16:09,072 --> 00:16:18,624
After moving the patient, immediately check the person before the
equipment. An airway or perfusion problem is more urgent than a neat-
looking dressing.

78
00:16:18,624 --> 00:16:31,368
Then trace the treatment: interface seal, connectors, tubing, clamp,
line site and securement. A device can move while the pump display or
drip chamber still appears unchanged.

79
00:16:31,368 --> 00:16:40,920
Reconfirm actual rate and delivered amount, especially with gravity
systems. Inspect for a pulled catheter or hidden swelling under a
blanket.

80
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:51,288
Document a changed route, stopped treatment or failed delivery
truthfully. Do not assume that the prepared amount equals the
administered amount after a disconnection.

81
00:16:51,288 --> 00:17:04,944
An adult is vomiting and cannot reliably respond. A family member
hands you an oral prescription and asks for the usual dose. There is
no demonstrated patient request and oral safety is not established.

82
00:17:04,944 --> 00:17:14,328
Begin immediate assessment and support the airway and breathing as
needed. Do not administer the oral product merely because its label
and dose are familiar.

83
00:17:14,328 --> 00:17:26,952
Determine the underlying emergency and applicable authority. A
separate indicated emergency treatment may exist, but you may not
invent an injectable conversion of the family's oral medicine.

84
00:17:26,952 --> 00:17:40,176
Explain why the route is unsafe, seek the appropriate support and
document the findings and plan. The safe decision integrates
physiology, consent, route and scope rather than using any one alone.

85
00:17:40,176 --> 00:17:51,288
At the school station, verbalize the specific route decision before
handling equipment. Identify the indication, authority and a reason
that this route could fail.

86
00:17:51,288 --> 00:18:02,160
Demonstrate preparation on the assigned training equipment, then have
the instructor check landmarks, needle choice, interface or connection
and medication-specific details.

87
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:15,528
Perform the current taught delivery sequence and immediately reassess
the intended effect and relevant harms. If a problem is introduced,
stop the implicated action and explain your recovery plan.

88
00:18:15,528 --> 00:18:27,384
Use the bilingual reading checklist to rehearse, but obtain the actual
school skill sign-off. A completed video or worksheet does not
demonstrate safe psychomotor performance by itself.
